  "attributionNote": "Traditionally ascribed to Shankara, and read as his hymn to the Goddess; a second, teaching the use of the circular diagram and the mantras, is regarded by many scholars as the work of a later Shri Vidya author.",
  "description": "The Saundarya Lahari is a hundred verses in praise of the Goddess as Tripurasundari: her form, her ornaments and her court, the vision of her beauty granted to the devotee, and, in its second half, the worship of her Shri Chakra and the mantras and the discipline of the Shakta tradition. It is at once a poem of devotion and a tantric manual in verse.",
  "historicalContext": "The hymn became the central devotional text of Shri Vidya and is recited wherever the Goddess is worshipped in the south of India; it is also the work through which the beauty of Sanskrit devotional verse has been most widely translated.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: all 103 verses of the Sanskrit text as GRETIL publishes it, streamed from GRETIL at read time. This is the original Sanskrit, not a translation.",
